<div class="incident-lens-summary" role="group" aria-labelledby="{{SUMMARY_PREFIX}}-title">
<h3 id="{{SUMMARY_PREFIX}}-title">Read the same incident through all three lenses</h3>
<div class="incident-lens-grid"><div class="incident-authority"><span class="record-kind">{{AUTHORITY_ICON}}<span>Authority</span></span><strong>What governs the work?</strong><p>Definition {{CASE_DEFINITION_VERSION}} supplies the criterion. The owner’s delegation permits the bounded investigation. Decisions for adoption, activation and incident closure have their own scopes.</p></div><div class="incident-provenance"><span class="record-kind">{{PROVENANCE_ICON}}<span>Provenance</span></span><strong>What produced this assessment?</strong><p>Snapshot {{CASE_SNAPSHOT}}, account {{CASE_ACCOUNT}}, its filters, output {{CASE_OUTPUT}}, method version {{CASE_METHOD_VERSION}} and session {{CASE_SESSION}} make the comparison inspectable. The evidence supports the omission claim within those conditions.</p></div><div class="incident-lifecycle"><span class="record-kind">{{LIFECYCLE_ICON}}<span>Lifecycle</span></span><strong>Where are we, and what may change?</strong><p>The definition is accepted. The fix is a candidate. The incident record is active and its response open. Expired information must be replaced with a newly checked view before further work.</p></div></div>
<p>The definition supplies the assessment’s criterion; the provenance trail identifies its basis; the lifecycle contract uses the required evidence and authorization to govern a particular change. A recorded change then has its own provenance. These checks continue through the investigation and any later response.</p>
</div>
